imagine you're on the muddy trenches on  the western front during world war one  you're wet and shivering and can't get  warm  your days are marked by sleeplessness  fear and cigarettes that are too soggy  to light  death is all around you  on the other side across from no man's  land are enemies soldiers with  advancements in technology your comrades  are mowed down by machine guns in  numbers beyond  imagination but you have no time to  mourn just fight lest you suffer the  same fate  but then amid unrelenting violence the  bloodshed and brutality suddenly  stop you put your weapons down cross the  enemy lines shake hands and pose for  photos with your foe even play a game of  soccer or football  to this day it remains one of the  strangest moments in the history of war  on christmas eve 1914 captain arthur  sullivan of the british army's royal  irish rifles was stationed in france on  the northern border with belgium the  guns were silent and he heard a german  voice say do not shoot after 12 o'clock  and we will not do so either  if you english come out and talk to us  we won't fire was it a trick there was  only one way to know for sure  an irishman dared to venture out to no  man's land the area between the opposing  army's trenches and was not shot at when  he reached the german side an enemy  soldier gave him a cigarette when he  returned safely his comrades clambered  out of the trench and went to meet the  germans halfway across no man's land  their superiors would be forgiven for  reacting with more caution brigade  headquarters cabled back warning it is  thought possible that enemy may be  contemplating an attack during christmas  or new year special vigilance will be  maintained during this period  at 8 30 pm that night captain  o'sullivan's commanding officer colonel  george laurie reported to headquarters  germans have illuminated their trenches  are singing songs and wishing us happy  christmas  compliments are being exchanged but i'm  nevertheless taking all military  precautions colonel laurie described the  germans as fine men clean and well  clothed  they gave us a cap and helmet badge and  a box of cigars  one of them states that the war would be  over in three weeks as they defeated  russia  despite thinking the war would be over  soon world war one would actually last  four years leaving 10 million soldiers  dead and about as many civilians  yet for a fleeting moment good appeared  to triumph over evil  instead of exchanging bullets sworn  enemies exchanged handshakes colonel  laurie later recalled you have no idea  how pleasant everything seems with no  rifle bullets or shells flying about  further down the line a german officer  who was a tenor in the berlin opera  began singing silent night in both  german and english encouraging his  comrades whose voices flowed over to the  british trenches  the contrast between the christmas  carol's message of peace and the  violence of war was striking  the same spirit of camaraderie sprung up  in various sectors along the front which  weaved more than 400 miles through  france and belgium  soldiers later wrote to their loved ones  expressing their disbelief over what had  occurred british soldier private  frederick heath described in his letter  home that he heard the germans say come  out english soldier come out here to us  for some little time we were cautious  and did not even answer  officers fearing treachery ordered the  men to be silent  but up and down our line one heared the  men answering that christmas greeting  from the enemy  how could we resist wishing each other a  merry christmas  even though we might get each other's  throats immediately afterwards  so we kept up a running conversation  with the germans all the while our hands  ready on our rifles  blood and peace enmity and fraternity  wars most amazing paradox  the night wore on to a dawn a night made  easier by songs from the german trenches  the pipings of piccolo's and from our  broad lines laughter and christmas  carols  not a shot was fired  the front page of the illustrated london  news showed a german soldier approaching  the british lines with a small tree as a  sign of a truce  very cautiously allied and german  soldiers climbed out of their trenches  and crossed no man's land where they  shared drinks with their enemies as  depicted in the film joao noel i don't  think anyone would criticize us for  laying down our rifles on christmas eve  don't worry it is just for tonight  the guns remain silent on christmas day  although there were accounts of fighting  in some sectors the imperial war museums  believes the truce extended along at  least two-thirds of the british held  trench line in southern belgium  german and british soldiers can be seen  standing together in photographs taken  on personal cameras that the soldiers  brought into the trenches with them it  was not easy for them to communicate  however what they did have in common was  an interest in sports a british private  described at least a couple hundred  soldiers kicking around a football this  was one of several impromptu soccer  matches played in no man's land  amongst the joy there was sadness as  both sides also used this opportunity to  recover the bodies of their fallen  comrades and give them a decent burial  which they couldn't do in the heat of  battle  headlines around the world spread the  news of the christmas truce which must  have seemed incredulous to the public  especially since british government  propaganda portrayed german soldiers as  ruthless barbaric monsters and yet the  british were seen mingling with the  enemy  a german lieutenant recalled  how marvelously wonderful  yet how strange it was  the english officers felt the same way  about it  thus christmas the celebration of love  managed to bring mortal enemies together  as friends for a time  for a time is right because the  following day they went back to shooting  each other  strict orders came down from the pike  and they had to retreat back to their  trenches this would be the only  widespread christmas truce during the  course of the entire four-year war high  commands on both sides prohibited  fraternizing with the enemy and the war  became increasingly bitter after the  introduction of poison gas the following  year and the number of casualties began  to pile up  the christmas truce of 1914 was such an  anomaly so hard to believe that over  time some people doubted that it even  happened despite all the evidence to the  contrary british machine gunner bruce  baron's father who later became a  prominent cartoonist depicted the truce  in his work in this cartoon he shows  himself taking a fancy to the buttons of  the german officer they agreed to do a  swap  in his memoirs baron's father described  the incredulity of it all  here they were  the actual practical soldiers of the  german army  there was not an atom of hate on either  side  even in the darkest hour good triumphed  over evil if just for a day  they had gotten to know the enemy and  realized they were more alike than they  thought  making these videos takes up a lot of my  time so i like to make sure the rest of  my day is as hassle free as possible and  that's down to the wallet that i use  so this is the wallet that i used to use  and this is the wallet that i currently  own an extra wallet it's clearly a lot  more sleek and simpler i actually  started using an extra wallet before the  company approached me to see if i was  interested in getting them to sponsor  some of my videos and i said of course  yes i absolutely love extra wallets  they're super simple to use all you have  to do is press this button  and then you can access to all your  cards i especially love this model in  particular because there's a spot to put  my air tag which is very convenient  because i don't have to spend time  looking for my wallet in the house i  know where it is at all times there are  so many models to choose from you can  check them out on their website exter  also sent me this really cool wallet  this is made of carbon fiber it's super  sleek and very minimalistic and i  absolutely love the design to go along  with it you can also get yourself a  smart tracking card exters runs on solar  power all it needs is three hours in the  sun and it lasts two months  if you'd like to try out an extra wallet  you can get twenty percent off if you  head to the custom link in my  description that's  shop.exter.com  newsthink  shop.extra.com  newsthink for 20 off thanks so much for  watching for news think i'm cindy palm
 

